WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.037 --> 00:00:01.120
SAAD ALI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.910
I'm here a KubeCon Seattle.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.670
And with me, I have
Dustin Kirkland,

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:09.011
Product Manager at Google
Cloud Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:09.011 --> 00:00:10.260
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: That's right.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.430
SAAD ALI: Dustin, you have
been with Google and Kubernetes

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:15.190
for a while.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.640
And we started with
the Google Cloud.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.267
The Kubernetes engine has
taken off in the cloud.

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:23.100
Since then, what have
we've been working on?

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.230
What's new with Google Cloud?

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.521
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah, I've
been really excited to work

00:00:27.521 --> 00:00:28.960
on bringing GKE On-Prem--

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.540
so Google's Kubernetes
Engine, which

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.990
we host a software, as a
service version of Kubernetes,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.730
in Google Cloud.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.540
Back in August of this year,
Google announced our intention

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.730
to bring GKE on-prem.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.730
SAAD ALI: And this is
completely new for Google.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:45.688
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: This is
new for Google-- yeah.

00:00:45.688 --> 00:00:47.490
We've never really
taken Google technology

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:52.116
and put it into a data
center at this kind of scale.

00:00:52.116 --> 00:00:53.490
From the background
that I have--

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:54.750
I have been working
with enterprises

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.315
and bringing Linux and open
source into those enterprises.

00:00:57.315 --> 00:00:59.520
And so it's been fun
and interesting to share

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.370
what I've learned over a
20-year career doing that.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.160
But here with Google, which has
some incredible technology that

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.200
typically is hosted
technology-- but doing

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.870
that on-prem in the
enterprise data center

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.010
is a different challenge.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:11.926
SAAD ALI: And why now?

00:01:11.926 --> 00:01:13.320
Why is Google
going into on-prem,

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.750
if this is not something
Google has done in the past?

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.430
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Yeah, it's response

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.780
to an incredible
market opportunity.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.690
There's a ton of workloads that
run much better in the cloud.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:26.930
And I think we've seen the
explosion of public clouds

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.600
in response to that.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.350
But increasingly, there are
workloads that cannot run

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.960
in the cloud, workloads
that have to run on-prem.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.210
And that is a little
counter-intuitive

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.990
to, maybe, some people
who are 100% cloud native.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.560
And so you think about something
like the retail industry

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.480
and the software that needs
to run inside of a store.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.820
There are physical
sensors inside

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.560
of the store that
needs to have software

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.000
running against those sensors.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.250
Now, those sensors can
feed data to the cloud,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.020
where decisions can be made.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.200
But the actual code that reads
the mag strip on a credit card

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.030
or the NFC or the chip
on a credit card--

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:03.930
that's code that has to run--

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:04.710
SAAD ALI: It needs to
be physically present--

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:05.070
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah--

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.111
SAAD ALI: --in the store.

00:02:06.111 --> 00:02:08.729
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: --within a
couple of feet of the card

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:10.110
reader-- the chip reader.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.930
The same goes for the sensors
inside of a restaurant,

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.870
inside of the
refrigerator or the store.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.079
All that's internet
connected nowadays.

00:02:18.079 --> 00:02:19.870
And I think, over the
next couple of years,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.437
you'll see the
in-store experience--

00:02:22.437 --> 00:02:24.270
the experience we have,
as a customer coming

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.964
into a brick-and-mortar
traditional retailer--

00:02:26.964 --> 00:02:29.130
we're really going to see
that enhanced and enhanced

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.780
by technology in the store.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.890
And across the board, every
retailer that I've met is

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.480
packaging their
own applications--

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.813
either applications they've
written or applications they

00:02:38.813 --> 00:02:41.340
purchase from a third-party--
packaging those as containers

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.860
and deploying them to
thousands of stores--

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.000
multi-node clusters in
thousands of stores.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.250
SAAD ALI: That sounds awesome.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:48.630
So what are some of
the benefits that folks

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.760
are going to get when
they have their clusters

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.200
managed by GKE On-Prem?

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.075
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Folks
being your IT administrator?

00:02:55.075 --> 00:02:57.660
SAAD ALI: Or retail
companies or whoever

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.130
is going to be
using GKE On-Prem.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.130
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right,
right-- so from a system

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:02.970
administrator--
an IT standpoint,

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.310
I think what you'll see
is Kubernetes managed

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:08.910
as appliances-- really,
appliances in the store,

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.630
where below the layer
of your application--

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.420
all of that's managed
as a service--

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.910
software as a service.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:17.940
It's remotely updated.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.050
It's remotely scaled, remotely
monitored, and remotely logged.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.610
And the applications that
sit on top are scaled--

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:31.210
are deployed-- are run in
a very seamless manner.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.460
SAAD ALI: That sounds awesome.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:36.102
So when are customers actually
going to get access to this?

00:03:36.102 --> 00:03:37.060
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Ha ha!

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.060
Good question-- 2019.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.190
2019 is the year of
retail really adopting

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.830
incredible software at scale.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:50.430
And I think it will change our
lives in ways that we're only

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.969
just beginning to see.

00:03:51.969 --> 00:03:53.010
SAAD ALI: That's awesome.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.860
And so moving forward, with
GKE going on-prem and all

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.840
of these things, is
Google going to continue

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:58.887
to invest in open source?

00:03:58.887 --> 00:04:00.220
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Oh, absolutely.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.220
I think it's in our DNA.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.500
I've certainly been around
the open source industry

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.480
as a contributor and
author of open source

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.130
projects for decades.

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.720
It attracted me in
my journey to Google.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:12.864
I've been with Google
for almost a year now.

00:04:12.864 --> 00:04:14.280
Google's commitment
to open source

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.890
is something that I'm
very proud of and--

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:19.631
I think-- increases,
in fact, over time.

00:04:19.631 --> 00:04:21.839
SAAD ALI: What are the
benefits of being open source?

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:23.627
Why continue that investment?

00:04:23.627 --> 00:04:25.710
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah-- I
think it's standardizing

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.040
on a common platform.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:28.860
There are certain
aspects of software

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:33.870
that, once commoditized,
make for a better playing

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.370
field for everyone.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:36.960
The differentiation
on certain things

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.672
that just don't benefit the
end-user for differentiation--

00:04:40.672 --> 00:04:41.880
it really doesn't make sense.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.920
And having a common
open source space

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.620
for that layer of the
infrastructure-- and that layer

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.852
started very low and
has slowly crept up--

00:04:48.852 --> 00:04:51.060
crept up in a good way,
because I think we've layered

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.950
on better and better services.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.020
The further to the top
of that stack you go,

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:00.140
the more commercialized or
productized and, in some cases,

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.570
proprietary that that is.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:05.264
I think it's inevitable,
though, that the layers continue

00:05:05.264 --> 00:05:05.930
creeping upward.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.360
And I think that's a good
thing for us as open source

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:10.587
technologists and for
the end-users benefiting

00:05:10.587 --> 00:05:11.420
from the technology.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:12.269
SAAD ALI: Yeah.

00:05:12.269 --> 00:05:13.310
What do you think about--

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:15.084
you have different
companies that are all

00:05:15.084 --> 00:05:16.250
contributing to open source.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.333
Sometimes, these companies
are seen as competitors

00:05:18.333 --> 00:05:19.460
to a certain extent.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:22.190
But open source is a place
where they actually collaborate.

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:22.520
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.228
SAAD ALI: What are
your thoughts on that?

00:05:24.228 --> 00:05:26.900
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: It's the
nature of the game, in fact.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:27.860
I think some of
the athletes that I

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.660
have the most respect
for are the ones who

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:32.240
have personal interpersonal
relationships with one another

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:33.080
off the field.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:34.760
On the field, it's grid iron.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:37.140
It's a battle.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.010
But to shake hands at the
end of the game and say,

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:43.012
we were bested this time and
we'll do better next time--

00:05:43.012 --> 00:05:45.470
that sort of collaboration, I
think, you see in open source

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:46.340
as well.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:47.270
We will compete.

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:50.690
We will compete in the
market and from a technology

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:51.840
perspective.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.400
But we'll also collaborate
around the open source.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:58.790
And I think that's a sign
of good healthy companies

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:00.980
and good healthy
business models.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:02.570
And on the flip
side, I think, when

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.710
you see organizations that
can't collaborate well,

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:09.980
that's a sign of something
unhealthy going on, perhaps,

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:11.030
in a business model.

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:12.290
SAAD ALI: Makes sense.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:14.509
And looking forward into
2019, what are the things

00:06:14.509 --> 00:06:15.800
that you're most excited about?

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.492
GKE On-Prem, of
course, but what else?

00:06:17.492 --> 00:06:18.950
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Yeah, so I'm going

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:20.075
to double down on that one.

00:06:20.075 --> 00:06:23.210
I think, GKE On-Prem
at tremendous scale

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:25.490
in the Enterprise
Data Center for sure--

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.400
that's where we're
currently engaged today--

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:32.030
so putting GKE On-Prem
into racks of servers

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:34.070
in a traditional data center.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:38.120
The other places at the edge,
in this retail sense-- thousands

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:40.370
of little data centers,
thousands of 5-node data

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:41.840
centers, right?

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.880
That is happening in 2019.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.230
And I'm excited to
be a part of that.

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:47.580
SAAD ALI: That is very exciting.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:48.080
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:49.310
SAAD ALI: So let's
talk about KubeCon.

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:50.601
This is not your first KubeCon.

00:06:50.601 --> 00:06:52.640
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
No, no, it's not.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.990
The last one in the US was in
my hometown of Austin, Texas.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:59.720
So it's incredible to see
it just double in size

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.090
from December a year ago.

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:05.570
Copenhagen was incredible--
had a blast there.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:08.360
Oh my god-- the
food in Copenhagen!

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:10.930
We ate our way through
the Michelin Guide

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.850
in Copenhagen. That
was a lot of fun.

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:17.570
But I also attended Berlin
a year and a half ago.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.700
And boy, that was a small
conference, compared to this.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.500
So it's grown up quite a bit.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.083
SAAD ALI: What are
you looking forward

00:07:24.083 --> 00:07:26.000
to this week at KubeCon?

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.981
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah-- I
think really educating more

00:07:29.981 --> 00:07:31.730
of the community--
what we're doing here--

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:35.000
but also learning from
the already advanced users

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.060
of Kubernetes,
hearing from them--

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:39.600
their use cases, if
they're a customer,

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:42.320
they're their pains, if they're
a developer trying to write

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:43.699
applications for Kubernetes.

00:07:43.699 --> 00:07:45.740
As a product manager, it's
incredible opportunity

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:49.219
to get feedback from our
customers and the community.

00:07:49.219 --> 00:07:50.260
SAAD ALI: That's awesome.

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:53.060
And finally, what's your
favorite KubeCon memory?

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:56.210
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
[LAUGHS] KubeCon memory.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:01.760
I got a Kubernetes hoodie,
six months ago, I guess.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.520
It was at the
Copenhagen KubeCon.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:08.450
And I was wearing that hoodie
a week ago-- last week.

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:10.970
And I took my car
into the shop to have

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:13.230
some repairs done on it.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:16.970
And in fact, the
mechanic that took my car

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:18.860
was wearing a GitHub hoodie.

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:21.020
And so I complimented
her on her hoodie.

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:23.270
And she told me, hey,
nice Kubernetes hoodie.

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:24.620
I like that.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:27.710
And I just thought,
what a cool world

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:31.280
we live in where someone who's
evidently writing some code

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.230
and using GitHub on the side
is also working on my car

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:39.409
and in the same
field of endeavor we

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:40.380
are with Kubernetes.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:40.900
And that's just--

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:41.660
SAAD ALI: That is wonderful.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:41.990
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:43.549
SAAD ALI: That is the
beauty of open source.

00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:44.000
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:45.230
SAAD ALI: All right, thank you
so much for your time, Dustin.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:45.800
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Yeah, you bet, Saad.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:46.010
Good to meet you.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:46.130
SAAD ALI: Have a good one.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:46.670
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah, yeah.

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:47.030
Good to see you.

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:47.863
SAAD ALI: Take care.

00:08:47.863 --> 00:08:49.780
[MUSICAL JINGLE]

